8.text:
(3, 5)

The white pair that has to be netted is M = (2,6)-(3,6).  
Before Black moves

Lib(M) = {(1,6),(2,5),(3,5),(4,6)}
Lib(B) = Lib{(3,4),(4,4)} = {(2,4),(3,5),(4,5)}

(3,5) is the only point that is a liberty of M and at the same time a liberty / extension of the central black B-group and it is orthogonal to (3,4) black and (3,6) white.

3,5
* reduces M to {(1,6),(2,5),(4,6)}, i.e. starts the net on the two-stone white group;
* enlarges B to {(3,4),(4,4),(3,5)} with liberties {(2,4),(4,5),(2,5)} and keeps the B–C complex alive;
* preserves the connection route to C = (1,7)-(2,7)-(3,7)-(4,7)-(5,5)-(5,6)-(5,7) via the follow-up (4,5).

After 3,5 White cannot run the pair out: e.g. White 2,5 is met by Black 4,6, White 4,6 is met by Black 1,6, etc., while Black retains the (4,5) link to the right-hand wall. 4,5 alone lets White take 3,5 first and (2,5) does not connect Black’s centre. Hence 3,5 is the vital net / survival point and the forced first move for the capture of (2,6)-(3,6).

